(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-physiology-master-exam-version-3"]={"title":"Physiology Master Exam — Form C — Units 6, 7, 12 & 13","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why does phosphorylating glucose to glucose-6-phosphate “trap” it inside a cell?","choices":["The charged phosphate group prevents it from crossing the membrane back out","It binds the glucose to the cell's DNA","It makes the glucose molecule much smaller","It immediately converts the trapped glucose into stored fat instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Adding a phosphate gives glucose-6-phosphate a charge that keeps it from recrossing the membrane, trapping it for use; tissues that export glucose need glucose-6-phosphatase to remove it. Phosphorylation does not shrink glucose, bind it to DNA, or turn it into fat. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 7"},{"q":"How does the number of white blood cells compare with red blood cells in the blood?","choices":["WBCs are roughly equal in number to RBCs","WBCs are about 700-fold fewer than RBCs","WBCs outnumber RBCs about 10-fold","WBCs are about 10-fold fewer than RBCs"],"answer":1,"exp":"With ~7,000 WBC/µL versus ~5,000,000 RBC/µL, WBCs are roughly 700-fold fewer than red cells. The other options misstate this large disparity. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 5"},{"q":"Segmentation contractions serve mainly to...","choices":["Rapidly propel chyme along the gut toward the colon","Trigger the intrinsic defecation reflex","Keep all of the gut sphincters tonically closed","Mix chyme with digestive secretions rather than propel it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Segmentation is a mixing movement that churns chyme with secretions and improves mucosal contact, with little net propulsion. Propulsion is peristalsis, tonic closure is a sphincter role, and the defecation reflex is a separate event. — GI Physiology, Slide 39"},{"q":"How does the biconcave disc shape of a red blood cell aid its function?","choices":["It maximizes mitochondrial packing for ATP production","It increases the number of heme groups the cell can hold","Its redundant membrane lets the cell deform to pass through capillaries","It stops the cell from ever being trapped and destroyed in the spleen"],"answer":2,"exp":"The biconcave shape provides excess ('redundant') membrane relative to volume, so the cell can bend and squeeze through capillaries narrower than itself. (A) is wrong — mature RBCs have no mitochondria. (B) is wrong — heme number tracks hemoglobin content, not shape. (D) is wrong — aged, stiffened cells are in fact removed in the spleen. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which TWO factors are chiefly responsible for the lung's elastic recoil?","choices":["Elastic tissue (collagen/elastin) and alveolar surface tension","Pulmonary blood flow and lymphatic drainage through the lung","Airway resistance and dead space","Surfactant and mucus production"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lung's recoil comes from its elastic fibers (collagen and elastin) plus the surface tension of the alveolar lining fluid. Resistance, dead space, blood flow, and mucus are unrelated to recoil, and surfactant actually reduces the surface-tension component. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 19"},{"q":"During intense exercise, the main reason muscle converts pyruvate to lactate is to...","choices":["produce extra ATP directly from the lactate","regenerate NAD⁺ so glycolysis can continue producing ATP","store oxygen for later use","make new glucose from pyruvate within the muscle fibre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Converting pyruvate to lactate reoxidizes NADH to NAD⁺, which glycolysis needs to keep running when oxygen or mitochondrial capacity is limiting. Lactate formation makes no extra ATP itself, stores no oxygen, and gluconeogenesis happens later in the liver, not the muscle. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 25"},{"q":"How do neutrophils and monocytes differ in their readiness to fight infection?","choices":["Neutrophils are immature on release; monocytes are fully active the moment they leave the marrow","Both remain immature until they are activated within the bone marrow itself","Neither can act until it has travelled to a lymph node and been primed","Neutrophils are mature and act immediately; monocytes mature into macrophages in the tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Neutrophils are mature cells that respond at once, whereas monocytes are relatively immature and become effective only after maturing into tissue macrophages. (A) reverses this, and (B) and (C) are incorrect. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 13"},{"q":"GI smooth muscle behaves as a functional syncytium because...","choices":["Each smooth-muscle cell is individually innervated by its own dedicated motor neuron","The cells physically fuse together into one giant multinucleated muscle fiber","Gap junctions electrically couple the cells, letting action potentials spread from cell to cell","It has no electrical connections of any kind between neighbouring cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gap junctions provide low-resistance electrical connections, so a signal spreads through the muscle as a unit. The cells are not each individually innervated, do not truly fuse into one fiber, and are certainly electrically connected. — GI Physiology, Slide 36"},{"q":"In which oxidation state must hemoglobin's iron remain to transport oxygen?","choices":["Ferrous (Fe²⁺); ferric iron cannot carry oxygen","Ferric (Fe³⁺); ferrous iron cannot carry oxygen","Either state carries oxygen equally well","Iron is not directly involved in oxygen binding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Oxygen is carried only when iron is ferrous (Fe²⁺); oxidation to ferric (Fe³⁺) yields methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen. (B) reverses the states, (C) is false, and (D) is wrong because iron is the oxygen-binding site. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 27"},{"q":"How does surface tension at the air–water interface affect the alveoli?","choices":["It increases lung compliance","It holds alveoli open by pushing their walls apart","It has no effect on alveolar stability","It draws alveolar walls together and favors collapse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Surface tension pulls the water-lined alveolar walls inward, tending to collapse the alveolus and lowering compliance. It does not push walls apart or leave stability unaffected — counteracting it is exactly why surfactant is needed. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 19"},{"q":"Beta-oxidation of fatty acids breaks them down into...","choices":["acetyl-CoA units that can enter the TCA cycle","amino acids","pyruvate directly","free glucose molecules released to the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Beta-oxidation sequentially cleaves fatty acids into acetyl-CoA units that feed the TCA cycle (and ketone-body synthesis). It does not yield glucose, amino acids, or pyruvate directly. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 69, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which trio of properties allows neutrophils and macrophages to reach a site of infection?","choices":["Diapedesis, ameboid motion, and chemotaxis","Agglutination, precipitation, and lysis","Opsonization, margination, and apoptosis","Phagocytosis, degranulation, and mitosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"These cells cross vessel walls (diapedesis), crawl through tissue (ameboid motion), and steer toward chemical signals (chemotaxis). (B) lists antibody actions, (C) mixes unrelated processes, and (D) lists effector/reproductive functions, not modes of locomotion. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 13"},{"q":"How is inactive pepsinogen converted to active pepsin?","choices":["Bile salts activate it once it reaches the duodenal lumen","Gastric acid converts it to pepsin, which then activates more pepsinogen (autocatalysis)","It is secreted from the chief cells already fully active","Enterokinase on the brush border cleaves it directly into its active pepsin form"],"answer":1,"exp":"Acid converts pepsinogen to pepsin, and the pepsin formed activates still more pepsinogen — an autocatalytic loop. Bile salts and enterokinase act on other substances, and pepsinogen is secreted inactive. — GI Physiology, Slide 83"},{"q":"How is oxygen bound to hemoglobin's iron?","choices":["By an irreversible covalent bond to the globin chain","By a loose, reversible bond at a coordination site of iron","By an ionic bond, so it is carried as ionic oxygen","By binding to the porphyrin ring, independent of iron"],"answer":1,"exp":"Oxygen binds loosely and reversibly to one of iron's coordination bonds and is carried as molecular (not ionic) oxygen, allowing loading in the lungs and unloading in tissues. (A) is wrong — binding is reversible; (C) is wrong — oxygen stays molecular; (D) is wrong — iron is the binding site. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 23"},{"q":"Functional residual capacity (FRC) represents the...","choices":["Maximum air inspired after a normal expiration","The maximum air expired following a maximal inspiration","Air remaining in the lungs after a normal (resting) expiration","Air moved in one normal breath"],"answer":2,"exp":"FRC (= ERV + RV) is the volume left in the lungs after a normal, resting expiration. The maximum inspired after a normal expiration is inspiratory capacity, the maximum expired after maximal inspiration is vital capacity, and one normal breath is tidal volume. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which is a normal function of cholesterol?","choices":["Functioning as a digestive enzyme","Acting as the body's main circulating energy currency","Carrying oxygen in the blood","Serving as a precursor for bile acids and steroid hormones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cholesterol is a membrane component and the precursor for bile acids and steroid hormones. ATP is the energy currency, hemoglobin carries oxygen, and cholesterol is not an enzyme. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 69, Slide 2"},{"q":"What is the role of Kupffer cells in the liver sinusoids?","choices":["They synthesize the plasma clotting factors released into blood","They store the iron released from senescent red blood cells","They produce the bile used for digestion in the small intestine","They remove nearly all bacteria from portal blood in a single pass"],"answer":3,"exp":"Kupffer cells are fixed macrophages that clear ~99% of bacteria from portal blood on a single pass. (A) and (C) are hepatocyte functions, and (B) is not the Kupffer cell's defensive role. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which is a physiological function of gastric acid (HCl)?","choices":["Converting pepsinogen to pepsin and providing a bacteriostatic environment","Emulsifying dietary fats so that they can be absorbed","Directly absorbing free amino acids across the gastric mucosal lining","Neutralizing the acidic chyme as it enters the duodenum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Gastric acid activates pepsinogen to pepsin, begins protein digestion, and limits ingested microbes (bacteriostatic). It does not emulsify fat, absorb amino acids, or neutralize duodenal chyme — that is bicarbonate's role. — GI Physiology, Slide 82"},{"q":"Where is the largest share of the body's iron located?","choices":["Stored as ferritin in the liver","Within hemoglobin","Within myoglobin","Bound to circulating transferrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Most body iron (~65%) is in hemoglobin. Liver ferritin stores, myoglobin, and transferrin-bound iron each hold much smaller fractions. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 24"},{"q":"Residual volume is the air that...","choices":["Enters the lungs during a normal breath","Can be forcibly inspired beyond a normal breath","Remains in the lungs even after a maximal expiration","Is exhaled during quiet breathing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Residual volume is the air that cannot be exhaled — it remains even after a maximal expiration, keeping alveoli from collapsing. Normal-breath air is tidal volume, extra inspired air is inspiratory reserve, and quiet-breathing exhaled air is tidal volume. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 31"},{"q":"Oxidative deamination of an amino acid removes the amino group, generating...","choices":["glucose","ammonia, which must be detoxified","a ketone body for use as fuel","a fatty acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Deamination strips the amino group off an amino acid, releasing ammonia (which the liver converts to urea). It does not directly yield glucose, a fatty acid, or a ketone body — those may come from the remaining carbon skeleton. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 70, Slide 16"},{"q":"Macrophages in the red pulp of the spleen are important for...","choices":["Producing erythropoietin to drive red cell output","Storing lymph fluid before it drains into the blood","Removing invading organisms, debris, and aged blood cells","Presenting antigen fragments exclusively to circulating B-cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Splenic red-pulp macrophages are richly exposed to circulating blood and remove organisms, debris, and senescent cells and platelets. Producing erythropoietin, storing lymph, and presenting antigen only to B-cells all misattribute their role. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 24"},{"q":"A major action of CCK is to...","choices":["Neutralize the duodenal acid by directly secreting bicarbonate into the gut lumen","Stimulate the parietal cells to secrete more gastric acid","Relax the gallbladder wall so that it stores a greater volume of bile","Contract the gallbladder and relax the sphincter of Oddi, delivering bile to the intestine"],"answer":3,"exp":"CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, sending bile and pancreatic enzymes into the duodenum. It does not stimulate acid, promote bile storage, or directly secrete bicarbonate. — GI Physiology, Slide 14"},{"q":"How does hematopoiesis shift in response to hypoxia versus infection?","choices":["Hypoxia drives white-cell output; infection drives red-cell output","Both conditions drive red-cell production equally","Hypoxia drives red-cell production; infection drives white-cell production","Neither condition changes hematopoiesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hematopoiesis adapts: hypoxia stimulates erythropoiesis (more red cells for oxygen delivery), while infection/inflammation stimulates white-cell production. (A) reverses these; (B) and (D) ignore the selective response. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 9"},{"q":"By Laplace's law (P = 2T/r), at equal surface tension a SMALLER alveolus...","choices":["Requires a larger pressure to keep from collapsing","Is inherently more stable than a large one","Requires a smaller pressure to keep from collapsing","Has no collapse tendency at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"With P = 2T/r, a smaller radius demands a higher pressure to stay open, so small alveoli are inherently less stable — which is why surfactant is essential. The other options reverse or ignore this radius relationship. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 22"},{"q":"The urea cycle, which occurs essentially only in the liver, serves to...","choices":["synthesize new proteins","convert toxic ammonia into urea for excretion","produce ATP directly","store the excess nitrogen away as glycogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"The urea cycle (ornithine–citrulline–arginine) packages toxic ammonia into urea for renal excretion; excess ammonia is dangerous partly because it depletes α-ketoglutarate and stalls the TCA cycle. It does not build proteins, make ATP directly, or store nitrogen as glycogen. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 70, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why are the tonsils and adenoids positioned in the throat and pharynx?","choices":["To filter aged and damaged red cells out of the blood","To intercept antigens entering from the upper respiratory tract","To store fully mature T-cells before releasing them","To produce the lymph fluid that drains the head"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lymphoid tissue is placed strategically; tonsils and adenoids intercept antigens from the upper respiratory tract. (A), (C), and (D) are not their function. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 39"},{"q":"Secretin is called “nature's antacid” because, in response to duodenal acid, it...","choices":["Directly buffers the acid that is already present inside the stomach lumen","Stimulates the antral G-cells to release still more gastrin","Stimulates pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion and inhibits gastric acid","Increases the rate at which the stomach empties acid into the gut"],"answer":2,"exp":"Secretin, released by duodenal acid, drives bicarbonate secretion from pancreas and bile ducts and inhibits gastric acid and gastrin. It does not buffer acid directly in the stomach, raise gastrin, or speed acid emptying. — GI Physiology, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why do individuals without functioning kidneys typically become anemic?","choices":["The liver overproduces EPO and suppresses the marrow","Residual EPO (mostly hepatic) is too little for normal red-cell production","The bone marrow is destroyed without a renal signal","Iron can no longer be absorbed at all without a hormone from the kidney"],"answer":1,"exp":"The kidney makes ~90% of EPO; the ~10% residual (mainly liver) is insufficient, so hematocrit falls toward 23–25%. (A) is wrong — hepatic EPO is too low, not excessive; (C) is wrong — the marrow isn't destroyed; (D) misstates iron absorption. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 15"},{"q":"Without surfactant, what would happen to a small alveolus connected to a larger one?","choices":["Surface tension would disappear","The large alveolus would collapse and empty into the smaller one","Both would remain perfectly stable","The small alveolus would tend to collapse and empty into the larger one"],"answer":3,"exp":"Without surfactant, the higher collapse pressure of the small alveolus (Laplace) drives its air into the larger, lower-pressure alveolus — atelectasis. The reverse flow, perfect stability, and disappearance of surface tension are all incorrect. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 23"},{"q":"Kupffer cells in the liver sinusoids function to...","choices":["remove bacteria and particulate matter (about 99% of gut bacteria) from portal blood","synthesize bile salts","store vitamin B12","produce the erythropoietin that drives red blood cell output in the marrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Kupffer cells are sinusoidal macrophages that clear ~99% of bacteria arriving from the gut via the portal blood. Bile-salt synthesis is a hepatocyte function, B12 storage is separate, and erythropoietin is chiefly renal. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 7"},{"q":"How is an ingested microbe digested inside a phagocyte?","choices":["The phagosome fuses with a lysosome, exposing the microbe to digestive enzymes","The microbe is expelled from the cell and then lysed outside it","Antibodies stored inside the cell dissolve the ingested microbe","The cell nucleus engulfs the microbe and degrades it internally"],"answer":0,"exp":"The phagosome fuses with a lysosome to form a phagolysosome, where proteolytic enzymes (and lipases in macrophages) digest the microbe. (B), (C), and (D) do not describe intracellular digestion. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 16"},{"q":"Pepsin works best under what conditions, and what happens as pH rises?","choices":["It is optimally active at alkaline pH (~8) and is inactivated as the pH falls toward acid","It is optimally active at acidic pH (~1.8–3.5) and is inactivated as pH rises toward neutral","It is equally active across the whole range of gastric and intestinal pH values","It requires bile salts to be present in order to function at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pepsin is optimally active in the acidic stomach (pH ~1.8–3.5) and loses activity as pH climbs toward neutral (irreversibly above pH 7–8). It is not alkaline-optimal, pH-independent, or bile-dependent. — GI Physiology, Slide 83"},{"q":"Thromboxane A2 released from platelets is especially important for which action?","choices":["Dissolving the fibrin clot once healing begins","Directly activating the intrinsic pathway","Inhibiting platelet aggregation to limit the clot","Constricting smaller blood vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Thromboxane A2 from platelets is particularly important for constricting smaller vessels. (A) describes fibrinolysis, (B) is unrelated to TXA2, and (C) is the opposite — TXA2 promotes, not inhibits, platelet activation. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why is the respiratory (alveolar) membrane extremely thin (~0.2 µm)?","choices":["To increase surface tension","To slow gas diffusion down so it can be regulated","To minimize diffusion distance and speed gas exchange","To store surfactant"],"answer":2,"exp":"At ~0.2 µm thick, the membrane keeps the diffusion distance tiny, allowing rapid gas exchange (Fick's law favors short distances). It is not there to raise surface tension, slow diffusion, or store surfactant. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 60"},{"q":"In hepatic bilirubin handling, which step is rate-limiting?","choices":["Uptake of free bilirubin into the hepatocyte","Conjugation with glucuronic acid","Binding of bilirubin to albumin in the blood","Excretion of conjugated bilirubin into bile"],"answer":3,"exp":"Of the hepatic steps — uptake, conjugation with glucuronic acid, and excretion — excretion of conjugated bilirubin into bile is rate-limiting. Albumin binding happens in the blood before uptake, not within the hepatocyte. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 14"},{"q":"How does fibrinogen leaking from damaged capillaries help contain an infection?","choices":["It clots in the tissue, walling off the area and limiting bacterial spread","It directly kills the bacteria by phagocytosing them in the tissue","It dissolves the cell walls of the invading bacteria","It attracts red blood cells to the site of the injury"],"answer":0,"exp":"Leaked fibrinogen clots in the interstitium, minimizing fluid exchange and 'walling off' the area so bacteria spread less. (B), (C), and (D) are not fibrinogen's role. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the role of bile salts in fat digestion?","choices":["They chemically break the triglycerides down into free fatty acids and glycerol","They emulsify large fat droplets into smaller ones, increasing the surface area for lipase","They transport absorbed fat directly into the portal blood for the liver","They neutralize stomach acid as it arrives in the duodenum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bile salts are emulsifiers: they disperse large fat globules into fine droplets, expanding the surface on which pancreatic lipase acts. The chemical breakdown of triglycerides is lipase's job, and bile salts neither carry fat into blood nor neutralize acid. — GI Physiology, Slide 137"},{"q":"What does prothrombin activator do?","choices":["It converts fibrinogen directly into fibrin strands","It converts prothrombin to thrombin in the presence of calcium","It cross-links adjacent fibrin fibers","It activates plasminogen to plasmin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Prothrombin activator, with Ca²⁺, converts prothrombin to thrombin. (A) is thrombin's downstream action, (C) is factor XIII's role, and (D) belongs to tissue plasminogen activator in fibrinolysis. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 40"},{"q":"If the respiratory membrane becomes thickened, gas exchange is impaired because...","choices":["The diffusion distance increases, slowing gas transfer","Surface area increases too much","The partial pressure gradient across it reverses direction","The solubility of the gases changes"],"answer":0,"exp":"A thicker membrane lengthens the path a gas must diffuse, and by Fick's law greater distance slows transfer (e.g., in edema or fibrosis). Thickening does not add useful surface area, reverse the gradient, or change gas solubility. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 62"},{"q":"A respiratory quotient (RQ) of about 1.0 indicates the body is burning mainly...","choices":["fat","protein","carbohydrate","a mix favoring fat"],"answer":2,"exp":"An RQ (CO₂ produced ÷ O₂ consumed) near 1.0 indicates carbohydrate oxidation; pure fat gives ~0.7 and protein ~0.8, with a mixed diet averaging ~0.8. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the main role of the complement system?","choices":["To produce the specific antibodies directed against each antigen","To enhance antibody and phagocyte actions and attack pathogen membranes","To present processed antigen fragments to helper T-cells","To generate the memory cells used on future exposures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Complement 'complements' antibodies and phagocytes — promoting clearance, driving inflammation, and attacking pathogen membranes. (A) is a B-cell role, (C) is antigen presentation, and (D) is a memory-cell function. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 53"},{"q":"The delivery of stored bile into the small intestine after a fatty meal is triggered mainly by...","choices":["Secretin, which contracts the gallbladder","Gastrin, which relaxes the sphincter of Oddi","Motilin, during fasting","CCK, which contracts the gallbladder"],"answer":3,"exp":"A fatty or protein-rich meal releases CCK, which contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi to deliver bile. Secretin drives bicarbonate, gastrin governs acid, and motilin acts during fasting. — GI Physiology, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the function of factor XIII (fibrin-stabilizing factor)?","choices":["It converts prothrombin into active thrombin","It initiates the extrinsic pathway","It covalently cross-links fibrin, strengthening the clot","It dissolves the clot after healing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Factor XIII in the plasma, activated by thrombin, covalently cross-links fibrin monomers and fibers, so the clot is weak without it. (A) is prothrombin activator's role, (B) is tissue factor's role, and (D) describes plasmin. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 40"},{"q":"The peripheral chemoreceptors of the carotid and aortic bodies are especially important for sensing...","choices":["Low arterial oxygen (PO₂)","Airway resistance in the bronchi","Pleural pressure","Lung compliance"],"answer":0,"exp":"The carotid and aortic bodies are the main sensors of low arterial PO₂ (they also respond to CO₂/H⁺). Compliance, resistance, and pleural pressure are mechanical properties, not what these chemoreceptors detect. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which vitamins are stored in the body to a significant extent?","choices":["Water entering the duodenum on its own-soluble vitamins such as C and the B group","Fat-soluble vitamins such as A and D","No vitamins are stored at all","Only vitamin C is stored"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) can be stored, notably A and D in the liver, whereas water-soluble vitamins (C and most Bs) are not stored significantly and must be supplied regularly. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 17"},{"q":"How do cytotoxic (killer) T-cells destroy their targets?","choices":["By engulfing the target cell and digesting it through phagocytosis","By walling the target cell off inside a mesh of fibrin","By producing large amounts of antibody that circulates in plasma","By secreting perforins that punch holes and delivering cytotoxic substances"],"answer":3,"exp":"Killer T-cells bind a target bearing its antigen, secrete pore-forming perforins, and release cytotoxic substances directly into the cell. Engulfing/digesting is a phagocyte's method, walling off with fibrin is inflammation, and producing circulating antibody is humoral immunity. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 59"},{"q":"In addition to acting on the pancreas, secretin stimulates the liver/biliary system to secrete...","choices":["Digestive enzymes","Intrinsic factor","Bicarbonate-rich fluid","Larger amounts of bile salts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Secretin stimulates the liver and bile ducts (and pancreas) to add bicarbonate-rich fluid that helps neutralize acid. It does not stimulate enzyme, intrinsic-factor, or bile-salt output. — GI Physiology, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why is the extrinsic pathway able to produce a clot faster than the intrinsic pathway?","choices":["It requires many more sequential enzymatic steps","It does not require calcium","It has fewer steps and can clot in under about 15 seconds","It bypasses factor X entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"The extrinsic pathway is 'explosive,' clotting in as little as ~15 seconds because it has fewer steps; the intrinsic pathway is slower (1–6 minutes). (A) is the opposite, (B) is false (Ca²⁺ is still needed), and (D) is wrong — both pathways activate factor X. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 47"},{"q":"Increased arterial PCO₂ stimulates the central chemoreceptors indirectly by...","choices":["Binding directly to the peripheral oxygen sensors","Constricting the airways","Reducing blood flow to the medulla","Generating H⁺ after CO₂ crosses into the brain/CSF"],"answer":3,"exp":"CO₂ crosses into the brain/CSF, forms carbonic acid, and releases H⁺; the central chemoreceptors actually respond to that H⁺. They are not oxygen sensors, and CO₂ does not drive breathing by cutting medullary blood flow or narrowing airways. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which nutrient produces the greatest thermic effect (rise in metabolic rate during digestion)?","choices":["Protein (up to ~30%)","Fat (~4%)","Carbohydrate (~4%)","All are equal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Protein has by far the largest thermic effect of food, raising metabolic rate up to ~30%, versus about 4% for carbohydrate and fat. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 73, Slide 10"},{"q":"The two branches of acquired immunity are...","choices":["Humoral (B-cell/antibody) immunity and cell-mediated (T-cell) immunity","Phagocytosis by neutrophils and activation of the complement system","Local inflammation and the systemic fever response","Physical skin barriers and the acidity of gastric secretions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Acquired immunity comprises humoral immunity (circulating antibodies from B-cells) and cell-mediated immunity (activated T-cells). (B) and (D) are innate mechanisms, and (C) lists non-specific responses. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 37"},{"q":"Why are pancreatic proteolytic enzymes secreted as inactive precursors (zymogens)?","choices":["Because they are only active in the stomach","To prevent the pancreas from digesting itself","Because acid is required to synthesize them","To allow them to be absorbed before activation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Proteolytic enzymes are stored and secreted as inactive zymogens (with a trypsin inhibitor present) so they cannot digest the pancreas that makes them. They are not stomach-specific, do not require acid for synthesis, and are not absorbed. — GI Physiology, Slide 103"},{"q":"What is the role of antithrombin III in controlling coagulation?","choices":["It combines with and inactivates thrombin not bound to fibrin","It converts circulating prothrombin into thrombin","It cross-links fibrin fibers","It activates the extrinsic pathway"],"answer":0,"exp":"Antithrombin III binds and inactivates the thrombin not already trapped on fibrin, limiting clot spread. (B), (C), and (D) describe pro-coagulant steps — the opposite of antithrombin III's inhibitory role. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 49"},{"q":"“Percent saturation” of hemoglobin refers to...","choices":["The absolute amount of O₂ in ml per 100 ml blood","The driving pressure for O₂ diffusion","The fraction of hemoglobin binding sites occupied by O₂","The number of red cells per microliter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Percent saturation is the proportion of hemoglobin binding sites carrying O₂ (a unitless fraction). The absolute amount is “content,” the driving pressure is partial pressure, and red-cell count is a separate measure. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which factor increases the metabolic rate?","choices":["Sleep","Decreased muscle mass","A warm, resting state after fasting","Increased thyroid hormone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Thyroid hormone — like exercise, greater lean mass, sympathetic activity, fever, and cold — raises metabolic rate. Sleep, less muscle, and a warm resting fasted state lower it. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 73, Slide 9"},{"q":"How can the body generate millions of distinct antibodies from only a few thousand relevant genes?","choices":["Each individual antibody is coded for by its own dedicated gene","Antibodies mutate at random following each separate infection","Complement proteins rearrange the antibodies already in the plasma","Hundreds of gene 'cassettes' (segments) recombine in many permutations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Diversity arises because hundreds of gene segments (cassettes) combine in vast numbers of permutations, yielding millions of specificities. (A) contradicts the segment model, and (B) and (C) are not the mechanism described. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 44"},{"q":"Pancreatic acinar cells store a trypsin inhibitor in order to...","choices":["Block any trypsin that forms prematurely inside the acinar cells and ducts","Speed up activation of the zymogens while still inside the pancreas","Neutralize the bicarbonate secreted by the duct cells","Activate chymotrypsinogen within the acinar cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Trypsin inhibitor neutralizes small amounts of trypsin that activate prematurely within acini and ducts, preventing autodigestion; overwhelming it contributes to pancreatitis. It does not accelerate activation, neutralize bicarbonate, or activate chymotrypsin. — GI Physiology, Slide 105"},{"q":"The flat upper plateau of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve means that...","choices":["Small drops in PO₂ at high levels cause large drops in saturation","Hemoglobin stays highly saturated across a wide range of higher PO₂ values","Hemoglobin cannot bind oxygen in the lungs","Saturation and PO₂ are unrelated"],"answer":1,"exp":"On the flat plateau, hemoglobin stays nearly fully saturated even as PO₂ varies over a wide high range — protecting O₂ loading if alveolar PO₂ dips. That is the opposite of large saturation swings, and saturation clearly depends on PO₂. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 80"},{"q":"The body's main temperature-control center is located in the...","choices":["cerebellum","the medulla's respiratory centre","hypothalamus (preoptic/anterior area)","spinal cord alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hypothalamus, especially the preoptic/anterior area with its heat- and cold-sensitive neurons, is the body's thermostat. The cerebellum, medullary respiratory center, and spinal cord are not the control center. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 74, Slide 5"},{"q":"How does passive immunity differ from active immunity?","choices":["It is generated by the person's own lymphocytes in the weeks after infection","It produces lifelong protection by laying down long-lived memory cells","It involves transferring preformed antibodies and is relatively short-lived","It relies on innate barriers such as intact skin and gastric acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Passive immunity comes from infused preformed antibodies (or activated cells) from an immune individual and lasts only ~2–3 weeks. (A) and (B) describe active immunity, and (D) describes innate immunity. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 63"},{"q":"Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion is important because it...","choices":["Directly activates trypsinogen to trypsin within the duodenal lumen","Emulsifies dietary fat so pancreatic lipase can act","Stimulates the stomach to secrete still more acid","Neutralizes acidic chyme, creating the pH (~7–8) needed for pancreatic enzymes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pancreatic bicarbonate neutralizes acid entering the duodenum, establishing the near-neutral pH at which pancreatic enzymes work best. It does not activate trypsinogen (enterokinase does), emulsify fat (bile does), or stimulate gastric acid. — GI Physiology, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which enzyme in red cells speeds the conversion of CO₂ and water to carbonic acid?","choices":["Carbonic anhydrase in the tubule","Carboxypeptidase","Lactate dehydrogenase","ATP synthase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Carbonic anhydrase in red cells rapidly converts CO₂ + H₂O to carbonic acid, which dissociates to H⁺ and bicarbonate. The other enzymes act on peptides, lactate, and ATP production respectively, not on CO₂ hydration. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 86"},{"q":"Sweat glands are stimulated to secrete by...","choices":["parasympathetic fibers releasing norepinephrine","sympathetic cholinergic fibers releasing acetylcholine","the somatic motor system","circulating insulin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sweating is driven by the anterior hypothalamus via sympathetic fibers that are cholinergic — they release acetylcholine even though they travel in sympathetic nerves. It is not parasympathetic, somatic, or insulin-mediated. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 74, Slide 8"},{"q":"After chylomicrons deliver most of their triglyceride to tissues, the remnants are...","choices":["Excreted in bile unchanged","Reabsorbed in the ileum","Taken up (cleared) by the liver","Converted back into bile salts in the gut"],"answer":2,"exp":"After lipoprotein lipase strips triglyceride from chylomicrons in the tissues, the cholesterol-rich remnants are cleared by the liver. They are not excreted intact in bile, reabsorbed in the ileum, or turned into bile salts in the gut. — GI Physiology, Slide 138"},{"q":"About 23% of CO₂ is transported...","choices":["Dissolved in plasma","Inside the circulating white blood cells","As free carbonic acid","Bound to hemoglobin (carbaminohemoglobin)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Roughly 23% of CO₂ is carried bound to hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin. Only ~7% is dissolved, most is bicarbonate, and CO₂ is not stored as free carbonic acid or within white cells. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 86"},{"q":"How is fructose absorbed, in contrast to glucose?","choices":["By facilitated diffusion (GLUT-5), needing no energy or sodium","By primary active transport, hydrolysing ATP directly","By secondary active transport coupled to sodium entry","By endocytosis across the enterocyte membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fructose crosses via GLUT-5 facilitated diffusion, moving down its gradient without energy or sodium coupling — unlike glucose and galactose. It is not actively transported or taken up by endocytosis. — GI Physiology, Slide 126"},{"q":"How does rising blood CO₂ lower blood pH?","choices":["CO₂ directly binds and removes bicarbonate","CO₂ combines with water to form carbonic acid, which releases H⁺","CO₂ destroys the buffering ability of the hemoglobin molecule","CO₂ has no relationship to pH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Dissolved CO₂ forms carbonic acid, which dissociates into H⁺ and bicarbonate, so rising CO₂ raises H⁺ and lowers pH. CO₂ does not remove bicarbonate directly or destroy hemoglobin buffering, and it is tightly linked to pH. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 86"},{"q":"The intrinsic defecation reflex is initiated when...","choices":["The internal anal sphincter contracts down considerably more firmly","The stomach has emptied itself completely of chyme","Feces enter and distend the rectum, triggering myenteric-plexus signals","Bile salts pass onward into the colon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rectal distension by feces triggers the intrinsic (ENS-mediated) reflex, which contracts the colon/rectum and relaxes the internal anal sphincter. Gastric emptying, sphincter contraction, and bile entering the colon do not initiate it. — GI Physiology, Slide 66"},{"q":"Tissue PO₂ is determined by the balance between...","choices":["Surfactant production and the alveolar surface tension it creates","Alveolar ventilation and dead space","Pleural pressure and compliance","The rate of blood flow (O₂ delivery) and the rate of O₂ consumption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Tissue PO₂ reflects the balance between O₂ delivered by blood flow and O₂ used by the cells — more flow raises it, more consumption lowers it. Surfactant, ventilation/dead space, and pleural mechanics govern the lung, not tissue PO₂. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 72"}]};
